1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,096
Welcome to episode two of the L-L-D Workshop, the low-level design series. Today: object-oriented

2
00:00:07,096 --> 00:00:08,616
programming in Java.

3
00:00:09,116 --> 00:00:13,830
By the end you'll explain the four pillars on one bank example, pick between

4
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:18,544
an interface and an abstract class, keep equals and hash code honest, and turn

5
00:00:18,544 --> 00:00:20,227
a one-line problem into classes.

6
00:00:20,727 --> 00:00:25,575
Keep the chapter two notes open. Each concept matches a section there, in order,

7
00:00:25,575 --> 00:00:30,076
and every listing runs. Pause and predict each output before I show it.

8
00:00:30,976 --> 00:00:36,487
Who is allowed to change a bank balance? Object-oriented programming, O-O-P, answers by bundling

9
00:00:36,487 --> 00:00:41,998
data with the only code allowed to modify it. That bundle is an object.

10
00:00:42,498 --> 00:00:48,010
Procedural programming separates the two. The data sits in one place, and free-standing functions

11
00:00:48,010 --> 00:00:50,766
anywhere in the program can change it.

12
00:00:51,266 --> 00:00:56,281
Cash on a table versus a vault with a teller. In O-O-P the teller,

13
00:00:56,281 --> 00:00:58,431
the object's methods, checks every request.

14
00:00:58,931 --> 00:01:03,579
Take the rule a balance never goes negative. If any code can write the

15
00:01:03,579 --> 00:01:08,227
balance, that rule is copied into every caller, and one forgotten copy is a

16
00:01:08,227 --> 00:01:10,219
bug. In O-O-P it lives once.

17
00:01:10,719 --> 00:01:15,899
And the design grows by adding classes, not editing old ones. In the interview,

18
00:01:15,899 --> 00:01:21,080
they watch you break a problem into objects, give each data and responsibilities, and

19
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:21,820
justify it.

20
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:28,220
Encapsulation asks who may change this data. For our bank: only the account itself

21
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:29,792
may touch its balance.

22
00:01:30,292 --> 00:01:36,128
Abstraction asks what a caller actually needs to know. The bank's transfer code needs

23
00:01:36,128 --> 00:01:38,630
three operations, not the account's insides.

24
00:01:39,130 --> 00:01:44,275
Inheritance asks what two types already share. A savings account and a checking account

25
00:01:44,275 --> 00:01:46,480
share a balance and its rules.

26
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:52,098
Polymorphism asks why one call gives different answers. Month end on savings pays interest,

27
00:01:52,098 --> 00:01:57,215
on checking it charges a fee. The first two pillars guard a single class.

28
00:01:57,215 --> 00:02:00,871
The last two let the design grow with new classes.

29
00:02:01,771 --> 00:02:07,285
A class is the blueprint: fields and operations, written once. An object is one

30
00:02:07,285 --> 00:02:12,799
concrete thing built from it. Account is a class. Ada's account S one, holding

31
00:02:12,799 --> 00:02:15,162
fifty thousand cents, is an object.

32
00:02:15,662 --> 00:02:20,864
A constructor runs when an object is created. It has the class's name, no

33
00:02:20,864 --> 00:02:25,323
return type, and one job: put the object into a valid state.

34
00:02:25,823 --> 00:02:30,975
Inside an object's methods, this means the current object. It tells a field from

35
00:02:30,975 --> 00:02:36,127
a parameter, as in this dot id equals id, and returning this lets calls

36
00:02:36,127 --> 00:02:36,495
chain.

37
00:02:36,995 --> 00:02:41,958
Static means the member belongs to the class, one copy shared by every object.

38
00:02:41,958 --> 00:02:46,922
A static method has no this, so it's for utilities like Math dot max

39
00:02:46,922 --> 00:02:47,631
and factories.

40
00:02:48,131 --> 00:02:53,888
Final means it can't change. A final variable is assigned once. A final method

41
00:02:53,888 --> 00:02:58,411
can't be overridden. A final class, like String, can't be extended.

42
00:02:58,911 --> 00:03:04,406
And a class-type variable holds a reference, like an address. Writing alias equals a

43
00:03:04,406 --> 00:03:06,761
copies the address, not the object.

44
00:03:07,661 --> 00:03:13,063
Step one. We open Ada's account with fifty thousand cents. The three-argument constructor is

45
00:03:13,063 --> 00:03:18,464
the canonical one that all others call. It validates, sets fields, and bumps a

46
00:03:18,464 --> 00:03:20,007
static counter to one.

47
00:03:20,507 --> 00:03:27,363
Step two. Kai's two-argument constructor chains with this, passing zero, so validation lives in

48
00:03:27,363 --> 00:03:32,749
one place. That call must be the first statement. Counter: two.

49
00:03:33,249 --> 00:03:39,266
Step three. Deposit returns this, so we chain: deposit one thousand, then five hundred.

50
00:03:39,266 --> 00:03:41,844
Kai now has fifteen hundred cents.

51
00:03:42,344 --> 00:03:47,698
Step four. alias equals a, then alias deposit one. Print a and it shows

52
00:03:47,698 --> 00:03:53,053
fifty thousand and one cents, and alias double-equals a is true. One object, two

53
00:03:53,053 --> 00:03:53,435
names.

54
00:03:53,935 --> 00:03:59,967
Step five. A negative opening balance throws before touching the counter, so it still

55
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:05,998
says two. Validate first, then change state. In the interview: I chain constructors so

56
00:04:05,998 --> 00:04:11,599
validation lives once, and avoid mutable static state, which is hidden global state.

57
00:04:12,499 --> 00:04:17,647
Encapsulation means an object hides its data and lets the outside change it only

58
00:04:17,647 --> 00:04:22,796
through methods it guards. Like a child-proof medicine cap. Step one, make fields private,

59
00:04:22,796 --> 00:04:24,635
and final where you can.

60
00:04:25,135 --> 00:04:31,171
Step two, expose behaviour, not setters. Deposit and withdraw, named after the business action.

61
00:04:31,171 --> 00:04:35,482
A set balance method is a public field in disguise.

62
00:04:35,982 --> 00:04:43,034
Step three, validate inside those methods. Deposit rejects zero or negative amounts. Withdraw rejects

63
00:04:43,034 --> 00:04:47,063
anything above the balance. The rule lives once.

64
00:04:47,563 --> 00:04:52,251
Step four, never hand out your live internal list. Return a copy or an

65
00:04:52,251 --> 00:04:55,599
unmodifiable view, which throws if someone tries to change it.

66
00:04:56,099 --> 00:05:01,128
Step five, for every field ask which code may modify it. If only this

67
00:05:01,128 --> 00:05:05,797
class, say so out loud. That makes a field list a visible decision.

68
00:05:06,697 --> 00:05:11,029
Imagine the balance were public. Any file in the code base could set it

69
00:05:11,029 --> 00:05:11,958
to minus five.

70
00:05:12,458 --> 00:05:16,701
If the bank then adds an overdraft rule, there's no central place to put

71
00:05:16,701 --> 00:05:18,520
it. You'd hunt through every caller.

72
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:23,165
A getter and setter for every field is the same problem: an anemic object.

73
00:05:23,665 --> 00:05:29,207
Now make it private. Someone tries to withdraw ninety thousand cents from an account

74
00:05:29,207 --> 00:05:32,770
holding forty thousand. Withdraw says no, and returns false.

75
00:05:33,270 --> 00:05:38,009
Every route to the balance goes through a method that can refuse. In the

76
00:05:38,009 --> 00:05:42,748
interview: encapsulation hides state behind methods, and those methods are the single home of

77
00:05:42,748 --> 00:05:44,441
every rule about that state.

78
00:05:44,941 --> 00:05:50,276
When not to bother: plain data carriers with no rules. A record gives those

79
00:05:50,276 --> 00:05:51,038
for free.

80
00:05:51,938 --> 00:05:57,253
Abstraction gives a caller just the operations it needs and hides how. Steering wheel

81
00:05:57,253 --> 00:06:00,670
and pedals: you drive without knowing about fuel injection.

82
00:06:01,170 --> 00:06:06,581
And an electric motor can replace the engine without changing how you drive. Encapsulation

83
00:06:06,581 --> 00:06:11,992
hides state. Abstraction hides design choices, so the short list of operations survives a

84
00:06:11,992 --> 00:06:13,538
full rewrite behind it.

85
00:06:14,038 --> 00:06:19,057
Step one, pick the smallest set of operations a caller needs. Step two, name

86
00:06:19,057 --> 00:06:22,283
them for what they do, like transfer, not how.

87
00:06:22,783 --> 00:06:27,489
Step three, put them on an interface or the class's public surface, keep the

88
00:06:27,489 --> 00:06:30,515
rest private, and let callers depend only on that.

89
00:06:31,015 --> 00:06:35,410
My test is one sentence: the caller does not need to know that. Every

90
00:06:35,410 --> 00:06:39,805
time you can honestly say it, something moves behind the surface and the design

91
00:06:39,805 --> 00:06:40,119
shrinks.

92
00:06:41,019 --> 00:06:47,956
Account has six public operations. Bank dot transfer uses exactly three: id, withdraw and

93
00:06:47,956 --> 00:06:48,451
deposit.

94
00:06:48,951 --> 00:06:54,364
It knows nothing about whole cents, the checks inside withdraw, or which subclass it's

95
00:06:54,364 --> 00:06:59,390
holding. Move the balance into a database table tomorrow and transfer doesn't change.

96
00:06:59,890 --> 00:07:05,239
The costs: a wrong abstraction is expensive to change, and a leaky one, like

97
00:07:05,239 --> 00:07:10,588
get hash map of seats, exposes what it should hide. Don't abstract before two

98
00:07:10,588 --> 00:07:11,734
concrete cases exist.

99
00:07:12,234 --> 00:07:18,614
In the interview: encapsulation hides and protects data with access control. Abstraction hides complexity

100
00:07:18,614 --> 00:07:23,627
and decisions behind a small set of operations. They work together.

101
00:07:24,527 --> 00:07:30,031
Inheritance lets a subclass start with everything its parent has, then add fields and

102
00:07:30,031 --> 00:07:35,535
methods or replace methods. Savings account extends account: an account with one extra rule.

103
00:07:36,035 --> 00:07:41,178
In Java a class has exactly one parent class but can implement many interfaces.

104
00:07:41,678 --> 00:07:47,946
Constructors aren't inherited. The child's constructor calls super first, then stores its own fields,

105
00:07:47,946 --> 00:07:49,737
like the interest rate.

106
00:07:50,237 --> 00:07:55,104
Inside an override, super dot method calls the parent's version, so you can reuse

107
00:07:55,104 --> 00:07:56,843
it and then extend it.

108
00:07:57,343 --> 00:08:03,265
Every class ultimately extends Object, which is why everything has to string, equals and

109
00:08:03,265 --> 00:08:04,111
hash code.

110
00:08:04,611 --> 00:08:09,024
The is-a test must hold in every situation. A car is not an engine,

111
00:08:09,024 --> 00:08:11,231
so a car holds an engine instead.

112
00:08:12,131 --> 00:08:18,482
Overriding means a subclass replaces a parent method. Rule one: same name, same parameter

113
00:08:18,482 --> 00:08:18,936
types.

114
00:08:19,436 --> 00:08:24,905
Rule two: the return type is the same, or a subtype, called a covariant

115
00:08:24,905 --> 00:08:29,202
return. Circle's copy method can return Circle where Shape's returns Shape.

116
00:08:29,702 --> 00:08:35,410
Rule three: access can stay the same or widen. Protected to public is fine.

117
00:08:35,410 --> 00:08:37,449
Public to protected won't compile.

118
00:08:37,949 --> 00:08:43,341
Rule four: checked exceptions, the ones you must catch or declare, can only shrink.

119
00:08:43,341 --> 00:08:44,882
Unchecked ones are free.

120
00:08:45,382 --> 00:08:50,703
Rule five: you can't override final methods, private methods, or static methods. A static

121
00:08:50,703 --> 00:08:55,263
method with the same signature in the child hides the parent's instead.

122
00:08:55,763 --> 00:09:01,885
Rule six: always write the at-override annotation. It catches typos. When not to inherit:

123
00:09:01,885 --> 00:09:08,008
just to reuse code, or when the child would disable a parent method. Java's

124
00:09:08,008 --> 00:09:14,130
Stack extends Vector, so a stack exposes insert at any index. In the interview:

125
00:09:14,130 --> 00:09:17,191
inheritance for genuine type families, composition otherwise.

126
00:09:18,091 --> 00:09:22,626
Pause and think. Can you override a static method in a subclass? Pick one

127
00:09:22,626 --> 00:09:23,922
before I go on.

128
00:09:24,822 --> 00:09:30,323
No. A same-signature static in the child only hides the parent's, and the declared

129
00:09:30,323 --> 00:09:31,894
type decides which runs.

130
00:09:32,394 --> 00:09:37,628
Polymorphism means one method call does different things depending on the object it lands

131
00:09:37,628 --> 00:09:42,114
on. One start button on a car, a washing machine, a laptop.

132
00:09:42,614 --> 00:09:47,905
Here's how, applied to our bank. Step one: declare monthly update on Account, with

133
00:09:47,905 --> 00:09:50,173
a default that returns no change.

134
00:09:50,673 --> 00:09:56,450
Step two: each child overrides it. Savings pays interest. Checking charges a fee unless

135
00:09:56,450 --> 00:09:58,513
the balance is high enough.

136
00:09:59,013 --> 00:10:04,235
Step three: the bank's month-end loop holds every account as plain Account and calls

137
00:10:04,235 --> 00:10:04,981
monthly update.

138
00:10:05,481 --> 00:10:10,786
Step four: at run time the J-V-M, the Java virtual machine, looks at the

139
00:10:10,786 --> 00:10:16,090
actual class of each object and runs that version. That's dynamic dispatch. No if

140
00:10:16,090 --> 00:10:18,743
statement ever asks which type it is.

141
00:10:19,643 --> 00:10:24,487
Here's the bank. Bank holds accounts and offers transfer and month end. Account has

142
00:10:24,487 --> 00:10:29,331
a private balance and final deposit and withdraw, so no child can weaken the

143
00:10:29,331 --> 00:10:29,677
rule.

144
00:10:30,177 --> 00:10:34,973
Savings account adds one field, a monthly rate of twenty-five basis points. A basis

145
00:10:34,973 --> 00:10:39,769
point is one hundredth of a percent, so that's a quarter of a percent.

146
00:10:40,269 --> 00:10:44,840
Checking account adds two fields: a fee of two hundred fifty cents, waived when

147
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:49,411
the balance is at least fifty thousand cents. And Ravi has a plain account

148
00:10:49,411 --> 00:10:51,370
that uses the parent's no change.

149
00:10:52,270 --> 00:10:58,629
Let's run it. Ada's savings opens with five hundred dollars, Kai's checking with eighty,

150
00:10:58,629 --> 00:11:04,535
Ravi's plain account with thirty. Each child constructor called super first. That's inheritance.

151
00:11:05,035 --> 00:11:11,136
Transfer one hundred dollars from Ada to Kai. Transfer only uses id, withdraw and

152
00:11:11,136 --> 00:11:15,930
deposit. Ada now has four hundred, Kai one eighty. That's abstraction.

153
00:11:16,430 --> 00:11:22,474
Now transfer nine hundred dollars from Ada, who has four hundred. Withdraw refuses, and

154
00:11:22,474 --> 00:11:28,519
the bank prints rejected, not enough money. The rule lived in one place. That's

155
00:11:28,519 --> 00:11:28,951
encapsulation.

156
00:11:29,451 --> 00:11:34,455
Month end. One loop, one method name. Ada's savings earns a quarter percent of

157
00:11:34,455 --> 00:11:39,102
four hundred dollars, exactly one dollar, so she has four hundred and one.

158
00:11:39,602 --> 00:11:44,810
Kai's checking holds one eighty, below the five hundred dollar waiver, so it pays

159
00:11:44,810 --> 00:11:48,158
a two dollar fifty fee, leaving one seventy-seven fifty.

160
00:11:48,658 --> 00:11:53,730
Ravi's plain account says no change. Three objects, three results, no type check, so

161
00:11:53,730 --> 00:11:58,802
a fourth account type needs no edit. Money is whole cents in a long,

162
00:11:58,802 --> 00:12:03,873
never a double, which can't store ten cents exactly. In the interview: I code

163
00:12:03,873 --> 00:12:08,945
against the parent type, each subtype overrides, the J-V-M picks at run time. And

164
00:12:08,945 --> 00:12:12,930
when you spot a long switch on type, point it out.

165
00:12:13,830 --> 00:12:19,648
Two kinds of polymorphism. Overloading means the same method name with different parameter lists,

166
00:12:19,648 --> 00:12:21,726
usually in the same class.

167
00:12:22,226 --> 00:12:28,201
The compiler chooses which overload to call, using the declared types of the arguments.

168
00:12:28,201 --> 00:12:30,761
That's why it's called compile-time polymorphism.

169
00:12:31,261 --> 00:12:36,003
Example: a calculator with add for two ints, add for two longs, add for

170
00:12:36,003 --> 00:12:39,052
two doubles, and add for any number of ints.

171
00:12:39,552 --> 00:12:46,090
Overriding means a subclass re-implements the same signature. It needs inheritance or an interface.

172
00:12:46,590 --> 00:12:51,894
The J-V-M chooses at run time from the actual class of the receiver. That's

173
00:12:51,894 --> 00:12:52,652
runtime polymorphism.

174
00:12:53,152 --> 00:12:59,605
Example: Circle's area overrides Shape's area. Remember: two methods can't differ only in return

175
00:12:59,605 --> 00:13:01,909
type. That's a compile error.

176
00:13:02,809 --> 00:13:07,653
The compiler tries three phases, and the first with a match wins. Phase one:

177
00:13:07,653 --> 00:13:12,498
exact match or widening, automatic conversion to a bigger type, int to long to

178
00:13:12,498 --> 00:13:17,342
double. Add two and three hits add int int. Add two-L and three has

179
00:13:17,342 --> 00:13:21,495
no long int version, so three widens and add long long runs.

180
00:13:21,995 --> 00:13:26,975
Phase two also allows boxing, which wraps an int in its object form, Integer.

181
00:13:27,475 --> 00:13:33,273
Phase three finally allows varargs, any number of arguments. Add one, two, three only

182
00:13:33,273 --> 00:13:39,071
matches the varargs version, so it prints six. Several candidates in one phase? The

183
00:13:39,071 --> 00:13:44,868
most specific wins, and if none is most specific, it's an ambiguous call and

184
00:13:44,868 --> 00:13:45,696
won't compile.

185
00:13:46,596 --> 00:13:51,583
Pause and think. We declare Shape s equals new Circle with radius one. Shape

186
00:13:51,583 --> 00:13:56,571
and Circle both have an area method, the calculator has a greet overload for

187
00:13:56,571 --> 00:14:01,558
Shape and one for Circle, and both classes declare a public field called kind.

188
00:14:01,558 --> 00:14:04,051
Predict all three before the next slide.

189
00:14:04,951 --> 00:14:10,007
s dot area prints three point one four. That's an override, so the J-V-M

190
00:14:10,007 --> 00:14:12,534
dispatches on the real object, a circle.

191
00:14:13,034 --> 00:14:18,027
calc dot greet of s prints greet Shape. Overloads are chosen at compile time

192
00:14:18,027 --> 00:14:22,662
from the declared type, and s is declared as Shape. The classic surprise.

193
00:14:23,162 --> 00:14:29,552
s dot kind prints shape. Fields resolve by the declared type, so a same-named

194
00:14:29,552 --> 00:14:35,943
child field just hides the parent's. Lesson: never hide fields. Use private fields and

195
00:14:35,943 --> 00:14:36,855
overridable methods.

196
00:14:37,355 --> 00:14:43,091
s dot copy returns a Circle, because Circle overrides copy with a covariant return.

197
00:14:43,591 --> 00:14:48,221
And the bug to name: equals taking a Point instead of an Object. It's

198
00:14:48,221 --> 00:14:52,520
an overload, so collections never call it, and at-override would have caught it.

199
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:59,111
An interface is a contract: methods a class promises to provide, without saying how.

200
00:14:59,111 --> 00:15:03,989
Like a wall-socket standard. Any plug fits any socket, whoever made them.

201
00:15:04,489 --> 00:15:09,643
A class can implement many interfaces but extend only one class. Fields in an

202
00:15:09,643 --> 00:15:14,429
interface are always public static final constants, so it holds no per-object state.

203
00:15:14,929 --> 00:15:21,247
Default methods, since Java eight, have a body implementers inherit. They let old interfaces

204
00:15:21,247 --> 00:15:24,857
grow, like Collection gaining stream, without breaking implementers.

205
00:15:25,357 --> 00:15:31,106
Static methods belong to the interface itself and aren't inherited. They're handy for factories.

206
00:15:31,606 --> 00:15:37,527
Private methods, since Java nine, are helpers for default methods, invisible to implementers.

207
00:15:38,027 --> 00:15:44,613
A functional interface has exactly one abstract method, so a lambda, a short unnamed

208
00:15:44,613 --> 00:15:50,730
inline function, can implement it. The at-functional-interface annotation makes the compiler enforce that.

209
00:15:51,630 --> 00:15:57,986
Our example: a Notifier interface for an online shop. Send is abstract. Email Notifier

210
00:15:57,986 --> 00:16:01,618
implements it: email to Ada, your order shipped.

211
00:16:02,118 --> 00:16:08,055
Because send is the only abstract method, Notifier is functional, so an S-M-S notifier

212
00:16:08,055 --> 00:16:11,024
is just a lambda. No class needed.

213
00:16:11,524 --> 00:16:16,749
Send all is a default method. It loops over recipients, Kai and Ravi, and

214
00:16:16,749 --> 00:16:20,107
calls send for each. Every implementer gets it free.

215
00:16:20,607 --> 00:16:26,142
Inside, send all calls clean, a private helper that strips the spaces from around

216
00:16:26,142 --> 00:16:28,910
sale starts today. Implementers can't see it.

217
00:16:29,410 --> 00:16:33,868
Notifier dot console is a static factory returning a console notifier.

218
00:16:34,368 --> 00:16:39,587
And a discount rule interface with one apply method. Ten percent off, then five

219
00:16:39,587 --> 00:16:44,807
dollars off, on a hundred dollars, which is ten thousand cents: nine thousand, then

220
00:16:44,807 --> 00:16:48,907
eighty-five hundred. The default method then joins two rules into one.

221
00:16:49,807 --> 00:16:55,964
Callers depend on what, not on a concrete class. Checkout depends on Notifier, not

222
00:16:55,964 --> 00:16:56,403
email.

223
00:16:56,903 --> 00:17:02,084
So adding push notifications needs no edit to checkout, and tests can pass a

224
00:17:02,084 --> 00:17:02,825
fake notifier.

225
00:17:03,325 --> 00:17:07,607
If two interfaces give the same default method, the class must override it, and

226
00:17:07,607 --> 00:17:10,360
can pick one with A dot super dot method.

227
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:16,470
When not: one implementation, no realistic second, no test double. That's an extra layer

228
00:17:16,470 --> 00:17:21,278
with no benefit. Add the interface when the second implementation shows up.

229
00:17:21,778 --> 00:17:28,077
Avoid fat interfaces that force empty methods. In the interview: services depend on interfaces

230
00:17:28,077 --> 00:17:32,577
like payment gateway, so I add implementations without editing callers.

231
00:17:33,477 --> 00:17:39,736
An abstract class can't be instantiated. It can hold fields, constructors, finished methods, and

232
00:17:39,736 --> 00:17:45,996
abstract methods subclasses must fill in, like a form with some fields pre-printed. An

233
00:17:45,996 --> 00:17:50,467
abstract class has instance fields. An interface only has constants.

234
00:17:50,967 --> 00:17:55,659
An abstract class has constructors. An interface never does.

235
00:17:56,159 --> 00:18:00,339
A class extends one abstract class but implements many interfaces.

236
00:18:00,839 --> 00:18:06,915
An abstract class expresses is-a with shared state. An interface expresses can-do, a capability

237
00:18:06,915 --> 00:18:07,783
or role.

238
00:18:08,283 --> 00:18:13,707
So use abstract classes for partial implementation in a close family, and interfaces for

239
00:18:13,707 --> 00:18:18,744
contracts, strategies, and test seams, the places a test swaps in a fake.

240
00:18:19,644 --> 00:18:25,586
Here's the worked example. Report is abstract. It holds a title field, which an

241
00:18:25,586 --> 00:18:30,678
interface couldn't, and a constructor. SalesReport calls super with the word Sales.

242
00:18:31,178 --> 00:18:36,114
Render is final. It glues header, body and a closing line, so the order

243
00:18:36,114 --> 00:18:41,050
can never change. A fixed skeleton with variable steps is the Template Method pattern.

244
00:18:41,550 --> 00:18:43,547
Header is concrete and overridable.

245
00:18:44,047 --> 00:18:50,029
Body is abstract. SalesReport supplies only this: total twelve thousand three hundred forty-five cents.

246
00:18:50,029 --> 00:18:56,011
Rule of thumb for the interview: start with an interface for the contract, and

247
00:18:56,011 --> 00:19:01,994
add an abstract class underneath when implementations share real state or a fixed algorithm.

248
00:19:01,994 --> 00:19:07,121
The J-D-K does exactly this: List, then Abstract List, then Array List.

249
00:19:08,021 --> 00:19:14,085
An enum, short for enumerated type, is a fixed set of named instances, like

250
00:19:14,085 --> 00:19:16,684
coin denominations. There's no seven-cent coin.

251
00:19:17,184 --> 00:19:23,494
String or int constants allow typos and invalid values. With an enum, only valid

252
00:19:23,494 --> 00:19:24,395
values compile.

253
00:19:24,895 --> 00:19:29,951
In Java an enum is a full class: fields, a private constructor, methods, even

254
00:19:29,951 --> 00:19:31,757
a method body per constant.

255
00:19:32,257 --> 00:19:37,881
There's exactly one instance per constant, so compare with double equals. And never save

256
00:19:37,881 --> 00:19:43,505
ordinal, the position number, to a database. Reorder the constants and every saved value

257
00:19:43,505 --> 00:19:45,915
means something else. Store the name.

258
00:19:46,415 --> 00:19:51,417
A switch expression that covers every constant needs no default, and when someone adds

259
00:19:51,417 --> 00:19:54,275
a constant, the compiler flags the missing case.

260
00:19:54,775 --> 00:20:00,673
The design rule: when variations differ only in data, like spot types, vehicle types

261
00:20:00,673 --> 00:20:05,307
or statuses, use an enum. When they behave differently, use classes.

262
00:20:06,207 --> 00:20:12,088
Example one, a vending machine. Each coin constant carries its value in cents through

263
00:20:12,088 --> 00:20:15,868
a private constructor: quarter twenty-five, dime ten, penny one.

264
00:20:16,368 --> 00:20:22,108
Insert two quarters, a dime and a penny. Summing each coin's cents gives sixty-one.

265
00:20:22,108 --> 00:20:23,338
No switch anywhere.

266
00:20:23,838 --> 00:20:29,356
Example two, an order lifecycle. Each status constant overrides a next method listing where

267
00:20:29,356 --> 00:20:34,873
it may go. Placed can go to paid or cancelled. Trying placed to shipped

268
00:20:34,873 --> 00:20:35,662
is refused.

269
00:20:36,162 --> 00:20:38,704
Placed to paid is allowed, so the order moves.

270
00:20:39,204 --> 00:20:42,468
Paid to shipped, then shipped to delivered. Both allowed.

271
00:20:42,968 --> 00:20:48,438
Delivered has no next states, so is final returns true, and the exhaustive switch

272
00:20:48,438 --> 00:20:53,907
labels it done. That's a tiny state machine in one guarded method. When not:

273
00:20:53,907 --> 00:20:59,377
values created by admins at run time, like product categories, can't be an enum.

274
00:21:00,277 --> 00:21:05,335
Composition builds a class by holding other objects and delegating to them. Has-a instead

275
00:21:05,335 --> 00:21:10,394
of is-a. A phone has a camera module. It isn't a kind of camera.

276
00:21:10,894 --> 00:21:16,317
Inheritance couples the child to the parent's internals, so a parent change can silently

277
00:21:16,317 --> 00:21:21,741
break it. That's the fragile base class problem. It's also fixed at compile time.

278
00:21:22,241 --> 00:21:28,177
The recipe, applied to a car. Step one: pick the behaviour to vary. Here,

279
00:21:28,177 --> 00:21:29,025
the engine.

280
00:21:29,525 --> 00:21:34,250
Step two: put it behind an interface, Engine, with one start method.

281
00:21:34,750 --> 00:21:39,510
Step three: hold it in a private field from the constructor, replaceable or fakeable.

282
00:21:40,010 --> 00:21:45,230
Step four: forward calls to it. Drive returns the model name plus engine dot

283
00:21:45,230 --> 00:21:50,449
start. Call replace engine with an electric motor and the same car now says

284
00:21:50,449 --> 00:21:54,551
electric motor hum. Inheritance can't swap a parent at run time.

285
00:21:55,451 --> 00:22:00,607
Imagine a set that counts attempted adds. Version one extends Hash Set and overrides

286
00:22:00,607 --> 00:22:05,764
add and add all. Add three letters with add all: the count says six.

287
00:22:06,264 --> 00:22:11,467
Why? The inherited add all calls add once per element, and our add counts

288
00:22:11,467 --> 00:22:16,298
again. The child depended on a parent internal detail nobody promised to keep.

289
00:22:16,798 --> 00:22:21,512
Version two, Counting Set, holds any Set in a private field and forwards to

290
00:22:21,512 --> 00:22:26,227
it. Its add all counts three and calls the inner set's add directly, not

291
00:22:26,227 --> 00:22:26,900
its own.

292
00:22:27,400 --> 00:22:34,137
Add a again: four attempts, size still three, with any Set. In the interview:

293
00:22:34,137 --> 00:22:40,873
I prefer composition, holding an interface-typed collaborator and delegating. It powers Strategy, Decorator and

294
00:22:40,873 --> 00:22:45,203
State. Inheritance stays for genuine is-a designed for extension.

295
00:22:46,103 --> 00:22:51,004
Pause and think. A Point class overrides equals but not hash code. Two points

296
00:22:51,004 --> 00:22:55,904
with x one and y two are equal. You add one to a Hash

297
00:22:55,904 --> 00:22:59,404
Set and ask whether it contains the other. What happens?

298
00:23:00,304 --> 00:23:04,322
The answer: contains returns false. Let's see why.

299
00:23:04,822 --> 00:23:10,675
Identity asks: do two references point at the same object? That's double equals.

300
00:23:11,175 --> 00:23:17,817
Equality asks: same value? That's equals. The default equals is identity, so value classes

301
00:23:17,817 --> 00:23:23,510
override it. Two printed copies of a book are equal, not identical.

302
00:23:24,010 --> 00:23:28,992
A Hash Set stores entries in slots called buckets. It computes the hash code,

303
00:23:28,992 --> 00:23:33,974
an int from the fields, jumps to one bucket, then compares the few objects

304
00:23:33,974 --> 00:23:35,041
there with equals.

305
00:23:35,541 --> 00:23:41,216
So equal objects must have equal hash codes. Otherwise the search starts in the

306
00:23:41,216 --> 00:23:46,890
wrong bucket. Unequal objects may share a hash code. That's a collision, allowed, just

307
00:23:46,890 --> 00:23:47,296
slower.

308
00:23:47,796 --> 00:23:54,380
Hence the rule: override equals, override hash code, on the same fields. Equals must

309
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:59,082
also be reflexive, symmetric, transitive, consistent, and false for null.

310
00:23:59,982 --> 00:24:05,348
Two points, both one comma two. Double equals is false: two separate objects.

311
00:24:05,848 --> 00:24:10,762
Equals is true, and their hash codes match, because hash code uses the same

312
00:24:10,762 --> 00:24:11,815
x and y.

313
00:24:12,315 --> 00:24:16,470
Add both to a Hash Set and the size is one. The set saw

314
00:24:16,470 --> 00:24:17,658
they're the same value.

315
00:24:18,158 --> 00:24:23,732
Broken Point overrides only equals. Its inherited hash code is identity-based, so an equal

316
00:24:23,732 --> 00:24:27,714
point lands in a different bucket and contains says false.

317
00:24:28,214 --> 00:24:34,543
Strings: hi double-equals a new string hi is false. Always compare strings with equals.

318
00:24:35,043 --> 00:24:40,784
Boxed numbers too. Integer caches only small values, so double equals works for ten

319
00:24:40,784 --> 00:24:46,525
and fails for a thousand. The design choice: value objects like Money compare all

320
00:24:46,525 --> 00:24:51,035
fields. Entities like User or Order compare by a stable I-D.

321
00:24:51,935 --> 00:24:57,393
An immutable object can't change after construction. A change makes a new object, like

322
00:24:57,393 --> 00:25:02,850
reprinting a receipt. It's thread-safe without locks and safe as a map key. The

323
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:08,307
checklist, on a Money class. Step one: class final, or a private constructor plus

324
00:25:08,307 --> 00:25:10,646
a static factory, Money dot of.

325
00:25:11,146 --> 00:25:16,582
Step two: all fields private and final. Money holds a Big Decimal amount and

326
00:25:16,582 --> 00:25:17,359
a currency.

327
00:25:17,859 --> 00:25:23,349
Step three: no setters. Plus returns a new Money. Five dollars plus two dollars

328
00:25:23,349 --> 00:25:26,094
gives seven, and the five is untouched.

329
00:25:26,594 --> 00:25:32,351
Step four: defensive copies. Copy mutable inputs in the constructor and never hand out

330
00:25:32,351 --> 00:25:33,585
internal mutable objects.

331
00:25:34,085 --> 00:25:37,486
Step five: don't leak this before construction finishes.

332
00:25:37,986 --> 00:25:43,791
A record, since Java sixteen, gives you this in one line: private final fields,

333
00:25:43,791 --> 00:25:49,595
a constructor, accessors, and equals, hash code and to string. But only shallowly. A

334
00:25:49,595 --> 00:25:53,326
list component can still change unless you copy it.

335
00:25:54,226 --> 00:25:59,187
Order is a record with an id, a list of items and a Money

336
00:25:59,187 --> 00:26:04,147
total. Its compact constructor checks the id isn't blank and replaces items with List

337
00:26:04,147 --> 00:26:06,628
dot copy of: a defensive, unmodifiable copy.

338
00:26:07,128 --> 00:26:11,624
The caller then adds sneaky to its own list. The order doesn't see it.

339
00:26:11,624 --> 00:26:12,588
Still just pen.

340
00:26:13,088 --> 00:26:17,818
With item is a wither: it returns a new order with pen and ink,

341
00:26:17,818 --> 00:26:20,183
total six fifty. The original is untouched.

342
00:26:20,683 --> 00:26:25,408
Try adding hack through the items accessor and you get an unsupported operation exception.

343
00:26:25,908 --> 00:26:31,795
And a record with the same components is equal. In the interview: value objects

344
00:26:31,795 --> 00:26:37,682
are immutable records. Entities whose state changes, like an account balance, stay mutable but

345
00:26:37,682 --> 00:26:38,102
encapsulated.

346
00:26:39,002 --> 00:26:45,293
A package is a named group of related classes. Access modifiers control who sees

347
00:26:45,293 --> 00:26:50,685
a member, making encapsulation compiler-enforced. Private: this class only, a desk drawer.

348
00:26:51,185 --> 00:26:56,701
No modifier at all means package-private: anything in the same package. The team room.

349
00:26:57,201 --> 00:27:04,134
Protected: the same package, plus subclasses in other packages through inheritance. The shared floor.

350
00:27:04,634 --> 00:27:10,789
Public: everyone, the lobby. The rule: start everything private and widen only when needed.

351
00:27:10,789 --> 00:27:16,944
Group packages by feature, like parking or payment, and keep helpers package-private behind a

352
00:27:16,944 --> 00:27:20,021
small public facade, one simple front class.

353
00:27:20,921 --> 00:27:26,556
A Wallet class lives in package core. A Premium Wallet subclass lives in package

354
00:27:26,556 --> 00:27:32,191
client. Let's try each field from the subclass. Owner is public, so anyone reads

355
00:27:32,191 --> 00:27:32,594
it.

356
00:27:33,094 --> 00:27:37,668
Bonus cents is protected, so the subclass can add a three hundred cent bonus.

357
00:27:38,168 --> 00:27:43,533
Audit count is package-private, so the subclass in another package can't touch it. That

358
00:27:43,533 --> 00:27:44,683
line won't compile.

359
00:27:45,183 --> 00:27:50,863
The balance is private. Not even the subclass can change it, only through add,

360
00:27:50,863 --> 00:27:51,675
which validates.

361
00:27:52,175 --> 00:27:58,280
Audit Log is a package-private class, so outsiders use the public Audits facade. Common

362
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:04,386
mistakes: everything public to be safe, and a whole machine-coding solution in one package.

363
00:28:04,386 --> 00:28:07,875
Use packages like model, repository, service and strategy.

364
00:28:08,775 --> 00:28:13,869
Generics let code work with any type while keeping compile-time type safety. A list

365
00:28:13,869 --> 00:28:18,599
of String holds only strings, with no casts that fail at run time.

366
00:28:19,099 --> 00:28:23,857
A bounded type parameter, T extends X, means T is X or a subtype,

367
00:28:23,857 --> 00:28:26,575
so you can call X's methods on it.

368
00:28:27,075 --> 00:28:32,321
A generic method declares its own type parameter. Max takes a list of anything

369
00:28:32,321 --> 00:28:34,194
comparable and returns the largest.

370
00:28:34,694 --> 00:28:41,590
Wildcards: question-mark extends T is a producer you read from. Question-mark super T is

371
00:28:41,590 --> 00:28:46,516
a consumer you write into. P-E-C-S: producer extends, consumer super.

372
00:28:47,016 --> 00:28:52,100
Type erasure: type arguments exist only at compile time. At run time a list

373
00:28:52,100 --> 00:28:57,184
of strings and a list of integers are the same class. So no new

374
00:28:57,184 --> 00:29:00,452
T, and no primitive type arguments: Integer, not int.

375
00:29:00,952 --> 00:29:05,798
And avoid raw types, a List with no angle brackets. They switch the checks

376
00:29:05,798 --> 00:29:06,144
off.

377
00:29:07,044 --> 00:29:11,861
In machine coding you'll want an in-memory repository. One generic Repository, with an I-D

378
00:29:11,861 --> 00:29:16,678
type and an entity type bounded to have an id method. Save Ada as

379
00:29:16,678 --> 00:29:19,087
u one and Kai as u two.

380
00:29:19,587 --> 00:29:24,197
Find by id u two gives Kai. Find u nine returns an empty Optional,

381
00:29:24,197 --> 00:29:27,820
a box that holds a value or nothing, instead of null.

382
00:29:28,320 --> 00:29:32,897
A product repository keyed by Integer holds a pen at a dollar fifty, a

383
00:29:32,897 --> 00:29:36,820
lamp at twenty-five, and a mug at eight. Max returns the lamp.

384
00:29:37,320 --> 00:29:41,716
Copy takes a list of integers as the producer and a list of numbers

385
00:29:41,716 --> 00:29:46,111
as the consumer. That's P-E-C-S. Note a list of Integer is not a list

386
00:29:46,111 --> 00:29:46,739
of Number.

387
00:29:47,239 --> 00:29:52,243
Saving a product into the user repository doesn't compile. The mistake is caught before

388
00:29:52,243 --> 00:29:53,673
the code ever runs.

389
00:29:54,173 --> 00:29:58,916
And comparing the runtime class of a string list and an integer list gives

390
00:29:58,916 --> 00:29:59,933
true. That's erasure.

391
00:30:00,833 --> 00:30:06,930
Two yardsticks. Cohesion is how closely the responsibilities inside one class belong together.

392
00:30:07,430 --> 00:30:12,389
High cohesion is good. Account's methods all work on the balance toward one purpose.

393
00:30:12,889 --> 00:30:17,884
The test: describe the class in one sentence without the word and. A class

394
00:30:17,884 --> 00:30:20,739
that manages accounts and sends emails fails it.

395
00:30:21,239 --> 00:30:24,745
Coupling is how much one class depends on other classes' details.

396
00:30:25,245 --> 00:30:30,701
Low coupling is good. Bank talks to Account through three operations, and checkout talks

397
00:30:30,701 --> 00:30:32,259
to a Notifier interface.

398
00:30:32,759 --> 00:30:40,007
So a change doesn't ripple. Everything today pushes the same way: encapsulation and access

399
00:30:40,007 --> 00:30:47,255
modifiers cut coupling, single-purpose classes raise cohesion, and interfaces and composition decouple callers from

400
00:30:47,255 --> 00:30:47,773
implementations.

401
00:30:48,673 --> 00:30:55,343
Object-oriented analysis and design, O-O-A-D, turns a written description into objects and links. Analysis

402
00:30:55,343 --> 00:31:02,014
asks what the system is and must do, in business terms: books, members, loans.

403
00:31:02,514 --> 00:31:08,656
Design asks which objects, with which responsibilities, deliver that behaviour, and how they're linked.

404
00:31:09,156 --> 00:31:13,923
Implementation is the code: array lists and hash maps. If you mention a hash

405
00:31:13,923 --> 00:31:18,689
map two minutes in, you skipped the two activities the interviewer wanted to watch.

406
00:31:19,589 --> 00:31:26,526
Four steps, in a fixed order, each using the previous one's output. One: find

407
00:31:26,526 --> 00:31:33,462
the objects. Two: define the relationships. Three: establish each object's interface. Four: make the

408
00:31:33,462 --> 00:31:33,958
design.

409
00:31:34,458 --> 00:31:39,330
Steps one and two are analysis. Steps three and four are design. Let's run

410
00:31:39,330 --> 00:31:44,203
all four on one sentence: library members borrow books, and each book on loan

411
00:31:44,203 --> 00:31:46,640
must be returned by a due date.

412
00:31:47,540 --> 00:31:54,176
Step one, find the objects, mostly nouns. Candidates: member, library, book, date, borrowed book.

413
00:31:54,176 --> 00:32:00,811
A date is a single value, so a field. The borrowed book becomes Loan,

414
00:32:00,811 --> 00:32:02,707
the one people miss.

415
00:32:03,207 --> 00:32:09,921
Step two, relationships: who holds whom, owns it, how many? The library owns its

416
00:32:09,921 --> 00:32:14,716
book records: composition. It registers members without owning them: association.

417
00:32:15,216 --> 00:32:19,477
Each loan points to exactly one book and one member, owning neither.

418
00:32:19,977 --> 00:32:25,516
Step three, interfaces, meaning the operations each object offers to other objects. Library can

419
00:32:25,516 --> 00:32:31,055
lend, take back, and say whether a book is out. Notice who answers is

420
00:32:31,055 --> 00:32:36,593
this book out: not Book, which knows nothing of loans, but Library, which keeps

421
00:32:36,593 --> 00:32:36,989
them.

422
00:32:37,489 --> 00:32:43,378
Loan answers is it late. Book gives title and I-S-B-N, Member a loan count.

423
00:32:43,378 --> 00:32:49,267
Everything else, including every field, is private. This is where encapsulation is actually decided.

424
00:32:49,767 --> 00:32:55,230
Step four: one class diagram, checked against the original sentence. The code comes from

425
00:32:55,230 --> 00:32:55,620
it.

426
00:32:56,520 --> 00:33:01,150
Here's the general rule. When two objects are linked and the link has data

427
00:33:01,150 --> 00:33:04,126
of its own, model the link as an object.

428
00:33:04,626 --> 00:33:08,454
A lent book has a borrower and a due date. Neither fits on Book,

429
00:33:08,454 --> 00:33:10,642
because a book on the shelf has neither.

430
00:33:11,142 --> 00:33:15,135
In a hotel it's the reservation, linking guest and room with dates.

431
00:33:15,635 --> 00:33:19,978
In a parking lot, the ticket: vehicle, spot and entry time.

432
00:33:20,478 --> 00:33:26,215
Students and courses: the enrollment. Spot the connection object early and the interviewer notices.

433
00:33:27,115 --> 00:33:33,557
The Java version, running. Loans last fourteen days. On day one Ada borrows Domain-Driven

434
00:33:33,557 --> 00:33:35,398
Design. Due day fifteen.

435
00:33:35,898 --> 00:33:40,703
On day two Kai asks for the same book. Library checks is out, and

436
00:33:40,703 --> 00:33:42,076
it's out, so refused.

437
00:33:42,576 --> 00:33:48,234
Kai borrows Design Patterns instead. Due day sixteen. Ada's loan count is one, and

438
00:33:48,234 --> 00:33:52,680
only Library can change it, because the count methods are package-private.

439
00:33:53,180 --> 00:33:58,267
Day ten, Ada returns her book. Ten isn't after fifteen, so on time.

440
00:33:58,767 --> 00:34:04,446
Day twenty, Kai returns his. Twenty is after sixteen, so the loan says late.

441
00:34:04,946 --> 00:34:10,294
Day twenty-one, returning it again: was not on loan. Every answer came from the

442
00:34:10,294 --> 00:34:12,203
object that owns the information.

443
00:34:13,103 --> 00:34:19,208
U-M-L, the Unified Modeling Language, is the standard notation. A filled diamond means ownership,

